        "math_academy_id": "q-73029",
        "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}-\\frac{1}{x-6}.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3}-\\frac{1}{x-6} & =\\frac{x-6-(x+3)}{(x+3)(x-6)}=-\\frac{9}{(x+3)(x-6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-6)=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=-3\\,,\\,x=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=-3$ and $x=6.$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
